erin: Heads-up — running into a weird case in the Go SDK when streaming SSE: long responses sometimes leave a partial JSON object in the buffer and the consumer goroutine stalls.

sam: ugh. do you have a minimal repro? which reader loop impl are you using?

erin: simple loop, bufio.Reader + ReadBytes('\n') expecting NDJSON but chunks split mid-object.

 erin: code:
 ```go
 r := bufio.NewReader(resp.Body)
 for {
     line, err := r.ReadBytes('\n')
     if err != nil { break }
     // assume line is a full JSON object
     var ev Event
     json.Unmarshal(line, &ev)
 }
 ```

sam: yeah that will break if the SSE chunk boundary == JSON boundary != newline. streaming side may send fragments. Use a decoder that can handle partial tokens or accumulate until a complete json is found.

maria: in our other SDKs we do a small state machine: append to buffer, try json.Unmarshal, if err -> check if "unexpected end" then continue reading, else surface error. Also enforce a max buffer length to avoid OOM.

alex: fwiw we saw stalls when backpressure + slow consumer means socket isn't drained and the server pauses sending; client Read may return small fragments repeatedly. Two mitigations: 1) use json.Decoder on a bytes.Reader wrapping buffer (handles streams), 2) set a read deadline and treat timeouts as "try again" rather than fatal.

jamie: example helper pattern we used:
 ```go
 buf := bytes.Buffer{}
 dec := json.NewDecoder(&buf)
 for chunk := range chunks {
   buf.Write(chunk)
   for {
     var msg Message
     if err := dec.Decode(&msg); err == nil {
       handle(msg)
     } else if err == io.EOF || strings.Contains(err.Error(), "unexpected EOF") {
       break // need more bytes
     } else {
       log.Printf("json decode err: %v", err); break
     }
   }
 }
 ```

erin: nice. two questions: 1) should the Go SDK expose a high-level streaming helper that does this? 2) is there an expected server-side SSE framing guarantee we can rely on?

sam: 1) yes please — open a small PR that adds a streaming.Decoder helper that returns an object channel and handles backpressure + max-buffer config. Keep defaults conservative. 2) No, we can't rely on newline-delimited JSON from all models/serving layers. Server may chunk arbitrarily.

maria: also add metrics: buffer_bytes, decode_errors, time_waiting_for_data. That helps prod triage.

ci-bot: test run for branch "streaming-decoder" passed: 42/42 unit tests, integration flaky on 1/3 runs.

erin: will draft PR today with tests reproducing fragmented-chunk case and a buffer cap. thanks everyone :thumbsup:
